While this is correct, i've _never_ witnessed "Stiege" used as meaning
a twenty in german, not in everyday speech and not in writing, outside
of my lexicon. Expressing 400 as "eine Stiege Stiegen" would get you
naught but a complete lack of understanding with well over 90% of
germans, i think.

Among the old counting expressions, only "Schock" and "Gros" might get
you _some_ accurate responses.

I wonder just how common the expression "score" is among native
english speakers[1]; i definitely didn't learn it in school when they
taught me english, and i've yet to encounter the strict numerical
meaning outside of roguelike games and "The Eye of Argon".

Not that it mattered to the topic at hand, "a score of scores" still
is explicitly giving a number, no matter how many casual readers will
accurately understand it.

[1] and i don't think that "scores of" really counts, even though it
is based on the numerical meaning: my (unreliable) impression is that
it's mostly used as a "classy" way of saying "hundreds of". And to
understand that it basically means "not enough to qualify for
'thousands of', but too many to easily count" you don't need to have
the faintest idea of how many items exactly make "a score".

